# ACT English, practice form 1: 5 passages x 10 questions (original items written to the enhanced ACT English specifications).
# Markup in passage text: {n|underlined words} marks the portion asked about in question n; {n|} marks a point (box only).
# Categories: POW = Production of Writing, KLA = Knowledge of Language, CSE = Conventions of Standard English.
NC = "NO CHANGE"
PASSAGES = [
{"title": "My Grandmother's Recipe Book", "paras": [
 "My grandmother never measured anything. When I asked her how much flour went into her bread, she would hold out a cupped hand and {1|say, \"About this much.\"} For years I assumed her recipes existed only in her memory.",
 "Then, while helping her move into a smaller apartment, I found a notebook wedged behind the spice rack. Its pages {2|was} stiff with flour and stained with tea. {3|Inside, there were} dozens of recipes written in three languages: Urdu, English, and a shorthand that only she could read.",
 "{4|Although} I had expected exact quantities, the notebook was full of notes like \"until it smells right\" and \"add more if the weather is dry.\" At first I was disappointed. {5|} Gradually, however, I realized that the notebook recorded something more valuable than measurements: it recorded {6|her judgment, her patience, and how she paid attention.}",
 "That winter, I tried to make her bread on my own. My first loaf was dense and pale; {7|my second loaf, which was the next one I made after the first,} was only slightly better. By the fifth attempt, I had begun to notice the things she had written about: the way the dough {8|changes} when it has been kneaded enough, the smell of yeast that is ready.",
 "Now when friends ask me for the recipe, I hand them a copy of the notebook {9|but explain} that the most important ingredient is not written anywhere. {10|}"],
 "qs": [
 {"c": [NC, "say \"About this much.\"", "say: \"About, this much.\"", "say, \"About this much\"."], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "A comma introduces a short direct quotation, and the period goes inside the closing quotation mark."},
 {"c": [NC, "is", "were", "has been"], "a": 2, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The subject is the plural \"pages,\" and the passage is in the past tense, so \"were\" is correct."},
 {"c": [NC, "Inside, it was", "Inside, there was", "Inside, there was being"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The verb must agree with the plural \"dozens of recipes\": \"there were.\" The other choices use singular or ungrammatical verbs."},
 {"c": [NC, "Because", "Since", "So that"], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "The writer expected exact quantities but found vague notes: a contrast, so \"Although\" fits. The other choices signal cause or purpose."},
 {"q": "At this point, the writer is considering adding the following sentence:\n\"I had hoped to copy the recipes into a neat, modern cookbook.\"\nShould the writer make this addition here?", "c": ["Yes, because it explains why the writer was disappointed by the vague notes.", "Yes, because it shows that the grandmother wanted her recipes published.", "No, because it contradicts the earlier claim that the recipes were in three languages.", "No, because it shifts the focus from the notebook to modern cookbooks in general."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "The sentence explains the disappointment: the writer wanted exact recipes to copy. It supports the paragraph rather than distracting from it."},
 {"c": [NC, "her judgment, her patience, and her attention.", "judging, being patient, and how she paid attention.", "her judgment, patience, and paying attention."], "a": 1, "cat": "CSE", "x": "Items in a series should be parallel: three nouns with \"her\" (judgment, patience, attention)."},
 {"c": [NC, "my second,", "my second loaf, made subsequently after the first,", "my second loaf, which followed the first and came next,"], "a": 1, "cat": "KLA", "x": "\"My second\" is concise; the other choices repeat the idea that the second loaf came after the first."},
 {"c": [NC, "changed", "has changed", "will have changed"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The sentence describes a general truth about dough (present tense), matching \"has been kneaded\" and \"is ready.\""},
 {"c": [NC, "but explaining", "but I explain", "but, explain"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"Hand ... but explain\" pairs two parallel verbs with the same subject, \"I.\" No comma is needed before the second verb."},
 {"q": "Which choice would most effectively conclude the essay by connecting to the idea introduced in the first paragraph?", "c": ["It lives, as it always did, in my grandmother's cupped hand.", "Bread has been made for thousands of years in many cultures.", "My friends usually prefer to buy bread from a bakery anyway.", "The notebook is now kept in a plastic sleeve to protect it."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "The essay opens with the grandmother's cupped hand as her \"measurement\"; returning to that image ties the ending to the beginning."}]},

{"title": "Mary Anning, Fossil Hunter", "paras": [
 "In the early 1800s, the cliffs near Lyme Regis, on England's southern coast, were constantly crumbling into the sea. For most residents, the landslides were a nuisance. For Mary Anning, {11|they were an opportunity}.",
 "Anning's family was poor, and she began searching the beach for fossils as a child, selling them to tourists to help pay the family's bills. Around 1811, she and her brother Joseph uncovered the skeleton of an {12|ichthyosaur, a} marine reptile that had lived more than 200 million years earlier. {13|Scientists, which were eager to study such creatures,} soon began visiting her shop.",
 "Over the following decades, Anning made discoveries that changed how scientists understood the history of life. She found the first complete plesiosaur skeleton and some of the earliest pterosaur fossils found in Britain. {14|Her} careful observations also helped show that certain stones, called coprolites, were fossilized dung.",
 "Despite these achievements, Anning received little formal recognition during her lifetime. Women could not join the Geological Society of London, {15|and} the scientists who bought her fossils often published descriptions of them without mentioning her name. {16|Nevertheless,} many of those same scientists relied on her expertise and visited her to discuss their work.",
 "Today, Anning is widely celebrated. {17|Statues, books, and films} tell her story, and her fossils can be seen in major museums. {18|Her life is a reminder that discoveries depend not only on luck but also on skill, persistence, and knowing where to look.}",
 "[1] Visitors to Lyme Regis can still walk the beaches where she worked. [2] Every winter storm exposes new fossils. [3] Fossil hunters, many of them inspired by Anning, {19|search the shoreline} in all kinds of weather. {20|}"],
 "qs": [
 {"c": [NC, "it was an opportunity", "they are an opportunity", "an opportunity is what they were"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"They\" refers to the plural \"landslides,\" and the past tense matches the paragraph."},
 {"c": [NC, "ichthyosaur; a", "ichthyosaur. A", "ichthyosaur a"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "A comma correctly sets off the appositive \"a marine reptile ...\" that renames \"ichthyosaur.\""},
 {"c": [NC, "Scientists, who were eager to study such creatures,", "Scientists who, were eager to study such creatures", "Scientists, eager to study such creatures, they"], "a": 1, "cat": "CSE", "x": "Use \"who\" for people; the nonessential clause is set off by a pair of commas."},
 {"c": [NC, "Their", "Its", "One's"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The observations are Anning's, so the singular \"Her\" is correct."},
 {"c": [NC, "but", "so", "or"], "a": 0, "cat": "KLA", "x": "Both clauses give reasons she lacked recognition, so \"and\" adds the second reason; \"but\" would wrongly signal contrast."},
 {"c": [NC, "Therefore,", "For example,", "Similarly,"], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "Scientists ignored her in print yet relied on her: a contrast, so \"Nevertheless\" fits."},
 {"c": [NC, "Statues, books, and films,", "Statues books and films", "Statues, books and, films"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "Commas separate the items in the series, and no comma belongs between the subject and the verb \"tell.\""},
 {"q": "If the writer were to delete the underlined sentence, the paragraph would primarily lose:", "c": ["a statement of the lesson the writer draws from Anning's life.", "an explanation of how Anning learned to find fossils.", "evidence that Anning's fossils are displayed in museums.", "a description of the scientists who visited Anning."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "The sentence states the broader lesson (skill, persistence, knowledge). It gives no new facts about museums, training or visitors."},
 {"c": [NC, "search, the shoreline", "searches the shoreline", "searching the shoreline"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The plural subject \"Fossil hunters\" takes the plural verb \"search\"; no comma separates a verb from its object."},
 {"q": "The writer wants to add the following sentence to the last paragraph:\n\"Some of them, like Anning, make finds that surprise the experts.\"\nThe sentence would most logically be placed:", "c": ["after Sentence 3.", "before Sentence 1.", "after Sentence 1.", "after Sentence 2."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "\"Some of them\" must refer to the fossil hunters named in Sentence 3, so the sentence belongs after it."}]},

{"title": "The Night Market", "paras": [
 "In many cities, when the offices close and the evening air cools, {21|empty parking lots transform into} night markets. Within an hour, rows of stalls appear, {22|each one lit by strings of bulbs, the smell of grilled corn drifting} between them.",
 "Night markets {23|have exist} for centuries, and they serve purposes that go beyond shopping. For small vendors, they offer a low-cost way to reach customers without renting a permanent shop. For residents, they {24|provide} a place to meet neighbors, try unfamiliar food, and enjoy the city after dark.",
 "Researchers who study urban life point out that night markets can also make streets {25|safer because} more people are present in public spaces in the evening. {26|Busy sidewalks give residents more \"eyes on the street,\"} an idea popularized by the urban writer Jane Jacobs, who argued that lively streets discourage crime.",
 "Running a successful market, however, {27|requires} careful planning. Organizers must arrange permits, provide lighting and waste collection, and balance the needs of vendors with those of nearby residents, {28|who may object to noise and traffic.}",
 "When these details are handled well, a night market becomes more than a place to buy things. {29|It turns an ordinary space into a shared one, where people who might never otherwise meet stand} in the same line for the same bowl of noodles. {30|}"],
 "qs": [
 {"c": [NC, "empty parking lots are transforming themselves to be", "empty parking lots, they transform into", "the transformation of empty parking lots into"], "a": 0, "cat": "KLA", "x": "\"Empty parking lots transform into\" is clear and concise; B is wordy, C adds a redundant pronoun, and D removes the verb."},
 {"c": [NC, "each one lit by strings of bulbs, the smell of grilled corn, which drifting", "each one is lit by strings of bulbs, the smell of grilled corn drifts", "each one lit by strings of bulbs, the smell of grilled corn drifts"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"each one lit ...\" and \"the smell ... drifting\" are absolute phrases that can follow the main clause. C and D create comma splices, and B leaves \"which\" without a verb."},
 {"c": [NC, "have existed", "has existed", "existing"], "a": 1, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The present perfect needs the past participle \"existed,\" and the plural subject \"markets\" takes \"have.\""},
 {"c": [NC, "provides", "providing", "had provided"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"They\" (night markets) is plural and the paragraph is in the present tense, so \"provide\" is correct."},
 {"c": [NC, "safer, because,", "safer; because", "safer. Because"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"because more people are present\" is an essential dependent clause, so no punctuation is needed. B adds stray commas, C misuses a semicolon, and D creates a fragment."},
 {"q": "Which choice provides the most logical and effective transition from the previous sentence to the explanation of Jacobs's idea?", "c": [NC, "Jane Jacobs was born in Pennsylvania,", "Many people enjoy walking at night,", "Parking lots are usually empty after dark,"], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "Linking \"more people present\" to \"eyes on the street\" connects the claim about safety to Jacobs's idea. The other choices add unrelated details."},
 {"c": [NC, "require", "are requiring", "have required"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The subject is the singular gerund \"Running,\" so the verb is \"requires.\""},
 {"c": [NC, "which may object to noise and traffic.", "whom may object to noise and traffic.", "who they may object to noise and traffic."], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"Who\" is the subject of \"may object\" and refers to people (residents)."},
 {"c": [NC, "It turns an ordinary space into a shared one, people who might never otherwise meet stand", "It turns an ordinary space into a shared one; where people who might never otherwise meet stand", "It turns an ordinary space into a shared one. Where people who might never otherwise meet stand"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"where people ... stand\" is a dependent clause that correctly modifies \"one\" after a comma. B is a comma splice; C and D separate a dependent clause as if it were complete."},
 {"q": "Suppose the writer's goal had been to write an essay explaining how night markets benefit cities beyond commerce. Would this essay accomplish that goal?", "c": ["Yes, because it discusses how markets help small vendors, connect residents, and can make streets safer.", "Yes, because it argues that night markets are more profitable than permanent shops.", "No, because it focuses mainly on the difficulties of organizing a night market.", "No, because it describes only the food that is sold at night markets."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "The essay covers community, safety and opportunity, which are benefits beyond buying and selling."}]},

{"title": "Learning to Fail Forward", "paras": [
 "Our school's robotics club had one goal for the regional competition: to build a robot that could pick up a ball and drop it into a basket. {31|} We had six weeks, a box of parts, and almost no experience.",
 "Our first design used a claw made of two metal arms. {32|It looked impressive, it dropped the ball} every time the robot moved. Our second design, a scoop, {33|worked better} but was too slow. With two weeks left, we were running out of ideas.",
 "That was when our coach asked us to stop building and start {34|writing}. She had each of us list every problem we had seen and every guess about its cause. Reading the lists aloud, we discovered that three of us had noticed the same thing: the ball was slightly heavier than the specifications said. {35|Our claw had been designed for a ball that did not exist.}",
 "{36|Armed with this information, a new gripper was designed by the team} using rubber pads that could hold the extra weight. {37|It had worked} on the very first test.",
 "We finished fourth at the competition, which was not the result we had hoped for. {38|Yet} I learned more from that season than from any trophy. I learned that a failure is often just information that has not yet been {39|read, and that} the most useful question after a mistake is not \"Who is to blame?\" but \"What is this telling us?\" {40|}"],
 "qs": [
 {"q": "Which choice, inserted here, would most effectively emphasize how difficult the club's task was?", "c": ["Only two members had ever built a robot before, and neither had built one that could grip anything.", "The competition was held in a large gymnasium.", "Robotics clubs are popular at many schools.", "The basket was painted bright orange so judges could see it."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "Lack of experience with grippers underlines the difficulty; the other choices give irrelevant details."},
 {"c": [NC, "Although it looked impressive, it dropped the ball", "It looked impressive it dropped the ball", "Looking impressive, it dropped the ball and"], "a": 1, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The original is a comma splice. \"Although\" makes the first clause dependent and shows the contrast between appearance and performance."},
 {"c": [NC, "worked better,", "working better", "had worked more better"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"Our second design ... worked better but was too slow\" uses a compound predicate; no comma is needed before \"but.\" D uses the incorrect \"more better.\""},
 {"c": [NC, "to write", "wrote", "written"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"Stop building and start writing\" keeps the two gerunds parallel."},
 {"q": "The writer is considering deleting the underlined sentence. Should the sentence be kept or deleted?", "c": ["Kept, because it states the key insight that explains why the claw had failed.", "Kept, because it shows that the coach had designed the original claw.", "Deleted, because it repeats information given earlier in the paragraph.", "Deleted, because it introduces a detail unrelated to the competition."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "The sentence draws the conclusion from the heavier ball: the design was built for the wrong weight. It is essential, not repetitive."},
 {"c": [NC, "Armed with this information, the team designed a new gripper", "Armed with this information, there was a new gripper designed", "A new gripper, armed with this information, was designed by the team"], "a": 1, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The opening phrase must describe the team, so \"the team\" should follow it immediately (avoids a dangling modifier)."},
 {"c": [NC, "It works", "It worked", "It has been working"], "a": 2, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The story is told in the simple past; \"It worked on the very first test\" fits. \"Had worked\" wrongly places it before another past event."},
 {"c": [NC, "Therefore,", "Similarly,", "In addition,"], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "Fourth place was disappointing, yet the writer learned a great deal: a contrast, so \"Yet\" fits."},
 {"c": [NC, "read; and that", "read. And that", "read and, that"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"I learned that ... and that ...\" joins two parallel noun clauses; a comma before \"and\" is acceptable here, while B, C and D break the structure."},
 {"q": "Which choice would best conclude the essay by connecting its lesson to the robot's story?", "c": ["Our robot had been telling us about the heavier ball all along; we just had to listen.", "Next year the competition will use a different kind of ball.", "Many famous inventors also failed before they succeeded.", "Our club now has twelve members instead of six."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "It links \"What is this telling us?\" back to the dropped ball, tying the lesson to the specific story."}]},

{"title": "The Science of Sourdough", "paras": [
 "Long before packaged yeast was sold in stores, bakers made bread rise using a sourdough starter: a simple mixture of flour and water {41|that has been left} to ferment. Today, many home bakers are rediscovering this ancient method.",
 "A starter is alive. It contains wild yeasts and lactic acid bacteria that feed on the sugars in flour. {42|As the yeasts feed, they release carbon dioxide gas, which becomes trapped in the dough and makes it rise.} The bacteria, meanwhile, produce acids that give sourdough its tangy flavor {43|and help keeping} the bread fresh for longer.",
 "Keeping a starter healthy {44|requires} regular feeding. A baker removes part of the starter and adds fresh flour and water, usually once a day. {45|If the starter is neglected,} the microbes run out of food, and the starter may develop an unpleasant smell or a layer of liquid on top.",
 "Because every kitchen contains a slightly different mix of microbes, no two starters are exactly alike. A starter made in a humid coastal city may taste different from one made in a dry mountain town. {46|For this reason,} some bakers describe their starters almost as if they were pets, {47|giving them names, and} passing them down through families.",
 "{48|Scientists have also become interested in sourdough.} Researchers have collected starters from bakers around the world to study {49|its} microbes, hoping to understand how communities of tiny organisms work together. {50|}"],
 "qs": [
 {"c": [NC, "that have been left", "which, has been left", "that leaving"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The singular \"mixture\" takes \"has been left.\""},
 {"q": "The writer is considering revising the underlined sentence to read: \"As the yeasts feed, they release gas.\" Should the writer make this revision?", "c": ["Yes, because the shorter sentence is clearer.", "Yes, because the details about carbon dioxide are explained later in the essay.", "No, because the original sentence explains how the gas makes the bread rise.", "No, because the original sentence shows that bacteria produce the gas."], "a": 2, "cat": "POW", "x": "The original explains the key process: trapped carbon dioxide makes dough rise. The revision would lose that explanation."},
 {"c": [NC, "and help keep", "and helping keep", "and to helping keep"], "a": 1, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"produce acids that give ... and help keep\" uses parallel verbs; \"help keeping\" is not idiomatic."},
 {"c": [NC, "require", "are requiring", "have required"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The subject is the gerund \"Keeping,\" which is singular, so \"requires\" is correct."},
 {"c": [NC, "Neglecting the starter,", "If neglecting the starter,", "When the starter neglects,"], "a": 0, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"If the starter is neglected\" clearly states the condition. B creates a dangling modifier (the microbes aren't neglecting), C lacks a subject, and D says the starter does the neglecting."},
 {"c": [NC, "However,", "In contrast,", "Instead,"], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "Because each starter is unique, bakers treat them like pets: cause and effect, so \"For this reason\" fits."},
 {"c": [NC, "giving them names and", "giving them names; and", "giving, them names and"], "a": 1, "cat": "CSE", "x": "\"giving them names and passing them down\" joins two parallel phrases with \"and\"; no comma is needed before \"and.\""},
 {"q": "Which choice provides the best transition into the final paragraph?", "c": [NC, "Sourdough bread is popular in many restaurants.", "Some bakers prefer packaged yeast because it is faster.", "Flour is made by grinding grains such as wheat."], "a": 0, "cat": "POW", "x": "The final paragraph is about scientific research, so a sentence about scientists' interest introduces it best."},
 {"c": [NC, "their", "it's", "there"], "a": 1, "cat": "CSE", "x": "The pronoun refers to the plural \"starters,\" so \"their\" (possessive plural) is correct."},
 {"q": "Suppose the writer's purpose had been to explain the process by which bread is baked in a commercial bakery. Would this essay fulfill that purpose?", "c": ["Yes, because it explains how yeast makes dough rise.", "Yes, because it describes how starters are fed each day.", "No, because it focuses on sourdough starters and the microbes in them, not on commercial baking.", "No, because it argues that packaged yeast should never be used."], "a": 2, "cat": "POW", "x": "The essay is about sourdough starters, their microbes and their care, not commercial bakery processes. It does not argue against packaged yeast."}]},
]
